# Building — reviewer 5 (failure-mode coverage)
VERDICT: FINDINGS
COUNT: 40
## Findings
1. Framework-Agnostic — engine-specific behavior can still ship outside session lifecycle — a separately maintained startup briefing or feature path touches neither launch, inject, nor resume, so the parity guards never inspect it and the codey under-briefing failure recurs.
2. An Instar Agent Is Always a Multi-Machine Entity — ordinary state can still be falsely classified as machine-local — an author supplies a valid taxonomy marker such as `hardware-bound-resource`; the report-only lint passes, and an unavailable semantic reviewer leaves consult memory fragmented exactly as before.
3. Cross-Store Coherence Is an Invariant — contradictory identity stores can persist on another fleet — the coherence audit exists only on the originating fleet, so a different installation can disagree until a consumer activates the weaker store.
4. Testing Integrity — dead code can still ship by escaping the undefined “significant” class — an author omits or declines that classification, so the unit/integration/E2E duties never attach and an uninstantiated sentinel remains green.
5. Test Identity Never Enters Production State — unrecognized test identity and non-identity residue remain writable — a fixture uses an ordinary-looking uid or writes workspace tokens/config outside the guarded registry, bypassing the marker-based refusal and recreating production pollution.
6. Scrape/Parser Fixture Realness — the `code=t` failure remains possible with a real but unchallenging fixture — one captured single-line pane satisfies the rule even though no fixture exercises hard wrapping; registration is also only a warning.
7. Zero-Failure — a discovered red suite can still remain ownerless — a developer abandons the branch or red CI run instead of pushing again; the gates block movement but assign no durable remediation owner.
8. LLM-Supervised Execution — subtly wrong deterministic output can still run unsupervised — the pipeline author labels it non-critical/tier0, and no independent classifier contradicts that choice.
9. Observability — a feature can still meter only the easy half of its loop — it publishes capture counters but omits surfaced/used/missed outcomes; no feature-to-metric census rejects the same partial-funnel instrumentation.
10. Expected Capacity Enforcement Is an Outcome, Not a Degradation — genuine loss can be silenced as expected enforcement — a contract classifies discarded essential fields as policy-compliant, the typed result reports success, and the structural lint cannot evaluate that semantic classification when review is absent.
11. Observable Intelligence — autonomous LLM work can remain unauditable through an unwrapped provider — a direct call outside the scanned funnel records neither resolved model nor acted/no-action verdict.
12. A Refusal Stays a Refusal — a rejection can become success at an unratcheted boundary — a new adapter or platform acknowledgement mapper outside `SessionRouter` flattens `rejected` to an accepted boolean and advances its offset.
13. Runtime End-to-End Proof — silent outcome loss remains possible outside the originating delivery canary — an outbound reply, another critical outcome, or another fleet fails while component health stays green because no generalized probe exercises it.
14. Migration Parity — an init-only installed artifact can still strand existing agents — a new built-in skill or installer path changes without joining `PostUpdateMigrator`; the eight committed migration shapes do not enumerate the new artifact.
15. Migration-Consumer Completeness — a canonical migration can omit the consumer that matters — the reply-authorization consumer is absent from `canonical-migration-contracts.json`, so all declared markers agree while that undeclared consumer keeps reading the retired authority.
16. Canonical Pipeline Operational Completeness — an accepted pipeline can still stop after deployment — its manifest and CI lifecycle test remain green while the production cadence is disabled or never scheduled, leaving accepted backlog without a live consumer.
17. Compaction Parity — required context can still disappear after compaction — a boot injector outside the recognized `*/session-context` fetch pattern, or one on the shrink-only legacy allowlist, is never re-injected.
18. Tiered Development — a risky change can still take the light path — the agent declares below the signaled floor, immediately receives Tier-1 treatment, and the cadence audit observes the under-tiering only after shipment.
19. Constitutional Traceability — weak constitutional fit can still ship unjudged — the semantic reviewer is unavailable, fails open, and leaves no record distinguishing outage bypass from a strong-fit verdict.
20. Friction Is a Spec — a hard-won workaround can still die in a transcript — the reviewer reminder is missed or unavailable and no artifact or gate records that the manual workaround occurred.
21. Notice + Solve Inefficiencies — the same standing inefficiency can remain normalized — agents repeatedly route around a rebase or CI bottleneck without filing it, and no signal distinguishes silent absorption from absence.
22. Bounded Notification Surface — the user can still be flooded inside one bounded topic — an emitter posts one message per element through the single Attention topic; the backstop and burst proof count created topics, not message volume.
23. Notices Route to the Alerts Topic, Never a New One — new installations can still recreate per-item topic spam — configuration selects the undated legacy `per-item` mode, which no guard restricts to pre-existing installs.
24. Conservative Outbound: Act, Don't Notify — narration can still ship enabled by default — a new proactive `sendToTopic` or `createAttentionItem` emitter passes because the promised disposition lint is not present.
25. No Unbounded Loops — the reaper/live-tail amplification class remains open — a new raw interval or retry ships without brakes because the loop-safety enforcement is still in progress.
26. Keep the Doorway/Model Map Current — a machine can silently retain stale routing pins — its scan stops, no liveness monitor notices, and the runtime keeps routing to the old model until an unrelated strict lint execution detects age.
27. Judgment Within Floors — an unbounded LLM arbiter can appear outside the named implementations — a new decision point bypasses `SpawnAdmission` and the duplicate reconciler, starts acting without shadow evidence, and can widen its action space unchecked.
28. Decision Provenance & Outcome Review — a judgment can still leave only scattered logs — an LLM decision path bypasses `JudgmentProvenanceLog`, and no funnel refuses the unlogged call or requires later outcome grading.
29. Stall Coverage Is Enumerated, Not Discovered — a known uncovered stall may still reach production — onboarding signs off a `declared-gap` cell with overseer acceptance, then that raw provider stop occurs during the allowed aging window.
30. Bounded Blast Radius — the host can still be exhausted by a non-LLM resource path — a worker pool, shell subprocess fan-out, or memory allocator bypasses the LLM-spawn funnel and its specialized lint.
31. Capacity Safety — swap/restart thrash can still ship through an unregistered controller — the class-closure gate is report-only and the universal self-action funnel does not exist, so a syntactically unrecognized emit never receives a convergence test.
32. Live-User-Channel Proof Before Done — the operator can still be the first tester — a dynamically mounted route is absent from the surface inventory or the gate runs in warn/dry-run mode, allowing the feature to reach the user without a live artifact.
33. Ownership-Gated Side Effects — non-owner side effects remain possible outside spawn admission — a notifier, rebind path, or new topic actor fires directly without the at-fire ownership proof enforced by `SpawnAdmission`.
34. Autonomous Throughput Floor — the manager can still remain the throughput bottleneck for hours — the flatline is exposed only on a posture endpoint and the mechanism intentionally neither alerts nor remediates, so an unread surface leaves the original hold intact.
35. Iterative Audit to Convergence — a one-pass audit can still be presented outside the canonical report path — no audit record is opened, so neither the convergence stamp nor its CI validator sees the premature stop.
36. A Metric Must Measure the Work, Not the Question — a falling count can still be credited as convergence — the trend travels in a message, commit body, or review prompt where no machinery records or varies the producing question.
37. A Decision That Can Block Must Live Where the Checks Can See It — an existing unwatched blocker can still drift — editing a file already declared blocking leaves the ceiling ratchet quiet, while the promised migration into the inspected language remains unfinished.
38. No Silent Degradation to Brittle Fallback — brittle fallback can regain gating authority through downstream reuse — an existing call remains classified advisory and permitted to degrade, then a caller begins treating its heuristic result as an action gate without creating a new recognized gating callsite.
39. An Autonomous Run Must Outlive Its Session — the original unregistered-run death path remains open — an operator-launched run is never registered, so it is still reaped as an ordinary session and never enters the guarded revival machinery.
40. One Failure Teaches Every Guard — the same inert-guard shape can still be rediscovered independently — the first incident is never voluntarily recorded, or its sweep records a match without requiring remediation, leaving sibling guards unchanged.
